From 8934205c3c7955bd7e1c9e055c8da3cd7a5cc112 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rautshivamxyz-beep <rautshivamxyz@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 03:37:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] v9.33.148 "The conversation answers for itself"

v9.33.147 gave every best-effort save a way to be heard, and fixed the six
sites that were throwing the answer away. The conversation store was not one
of them, and it is the one file in the app the user cannot re-type.

ChatStore.save called NcieFiles.write(...) and dropped the Boolean it
answered. v9.33.90 had already made that call stop throwing, so nothing
anywhere could report a failed save: a full disk, a storage fault or a
filesystem that refused the rename lost an entire conversation in silence,
and the screen went on showing it exactly as if it were on the disk. The
`catch` around the call was dead code of the same kind v9.33.147 removed
from six other files.

save() now answers. The JSON is still built inside the try, because building
it can throw; the write has moved out of it, because it cannot. The answer
goes through NcieFiles.writeOrNote, so a failed conversation save lands in
the swallowed-failure tally the health screen reads, and it comes back to
the caller.

Two screens were claiming things that a failed save made untrue. "Message
deleted" was shown whether or not the deletion reached the disk, so the
message returned at the next launch after the user had been told it was
gone. A rename was accepted and shown in the list, then silently reverted on
the next open. Both now ask first: the delete says "Message deleted" only
when it was, and a rename that could not be saved puts the old name back and
says why instead of showing a name that is not on the phone.

The wording lives in a pure ChatStore.saveNotice(saved), so the half that
needs no Context or filesystem is pinned by three JVM tests. Nine verify
checks hold the plumbing.
